      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "This is a secondary analysis of a prospective cohort of 295 patients with acute low back pain presenting to 31 primary care physicians in North Carolina."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study examines the hypothesis that dissatisfaction with job tasks and lack of social support from coworkers and supervisor are associated with poorer low back pain outcomes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Psychosocial work characteristics are thought to be associated with the occurrence, report, and development of long-term disability from low back pain, but the studies are inconclusive. METHOD.: Three psychosocial work characteristics were separately compared to these outcomes: time to functional recovery, attainment of complete recovery, and clinically relevant change on the Modified Roland Scale."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Relative to subjects with more social support from coworkers, subjects with less social support from coworkers have 1.55 (95% CI = 1.04-2.34) times the risk of not attaining complete recovery from low back pain at 8 weeks. For all other outcomes evaluated in this study, there was not an association with the psychosocial work characteristics. Biomechanical demands were found not to modify this association."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This analysis provides evidence that social support in the workplace from coworkers but not social support from a supervisor or job task satisfaction are likely targets for intervention."
        }
      ]},
